# Graph Neural Networks — Rehearsal Speaker Script

**Total Runtime: 10:00 (600s).**
Deck: [`gnn-deck.html`](file:///c:/Users/user/Desktop/presentation/gnn-deck.html) — open in Chrome, press `F` for fullscreen, `S` for presenter notes drawer, `T` to toggle the 10:00 pacing timer.

---

## Storytelling & Delivery Principles

1. **Misconception Before Explanation (Veritasium Rule 1)**:
   Never start by announcing "Today I will talk about Graph Neural Networks." Start by giving the audience a puzzle where their initial mental model fails.
2. **Question Before Solution (Veritasium Rule 2)**:
   Ask the question out loud before revealing the concept. Let the question hang in the air so the audience actively craves the answer.
3. **Causal Progression (The Veritasium Thread)**:
   Every block must directly create the question answered by the next block:
   *CNN works on grids &rarr; our data has no grid &rarr; obvious fixes fail &rarr; relationships contain information &rarr; GNN &rarr; step-by-step mechanism &rarr; real-world payoff.*
4. **The Faculty Rule (What Judges Must Leave Remembering)**:
   Do not drown the room in tensor algebra. Ensure every judge walks out remembering three clear ideas:
   - **CNNs exploit grids.**
   - **GNNs exploit relationships.**
   - **Gather &rarr; Aggregate &rarr; Update.**

---

## 0:00 — Scene 1 · Cold Open: Trust the Person, or Trust the Network? `45s`

**[On Screen: A single pinned suspect card in darkness. No title. No GNN mention.]**

> "Let me give you a very simple problem.
>
> You have to decide whether this person is trustworthy.
>
> Here is everything we know about them.
>
> Twenty-four years old.
> Seventeen posts.
> Lives in Dhaka.
>
> Can you decide?"

*(Pause. Count to two. Look around the room.)*

> "Probably not. There just isn't enough information here.
>
> So let me give you one more piece of data."

**[Fragment trigger: Red strings snap outward to connected nodes: VERIFIED BANK at the top, two SCAM ACCOUNTS below.]**

> "Look at the people around them.
>
> Now the answer suddenly feels very different."

*(Pause.)*

> "The interesting thing is...
> we didn't learn a single new fact about the person.
>
> We learned about the people they are connected to.
>
> What if the person itself isn't the information?
> What if the **connections** are?"

---

## 0:45 — Scene 2 · The CNN Example `35s`

**[Cut to an image on screen.]**

> "Now let's forget people for a moment.
>
> Suppose I give this image to a neural network.
> How could it recognize what's in this picture?"

**[Fragment trigger: The image decomposes into local 3x3 pixel patches.]**

> "It doesn't need to understand the entire picture all at once.
> It can look at small, local regions.
>
> Pixels... combine into edges.
> Edges combine into patterns.
> Patterns combine into shapes.
> And shapes combine into an object."

**[Fragment trigger: Object recognized.]**

> "And there is a reason this works so well.
> This image has structure.
> Every pixel has a predictable neighbourhood."

---

## 1:20 — Scene 3 · Name the CNN `20s`

**[Typographic reveal: Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)]**

> "This approach has a name you already know.
>
> A **Convolutional Neural Network**. CNN.
>
> But here is the critical part to remember:
> CNN works because the world we gave it has an obvious, rigid arrangement."

---

## 1:40 — Scene 4 · Break the Assumption: Grid vs. Graph `25s`

**[Side-by-side comparison: A neat rectangular pixel grid on the left; an irregular messy graph on the right.]**

> "Now imagine the data doesn't look like this."

**[Point to the grid.]**

> "In a CNN, pixel number 37 has a predictable neighbor on the left, right, top, and bottom."

**[Point to the graph.]**

> "Now look over here.
>
> What is pixel number 37 in this graph?
>
> There is no pixel 37.
> There are no rows. No columns. No left-to-right order.
>
> There are only things... and relationships between those things.
>
> So what do we do?"

---

## 2:05 — Scene 5 · Obvious Solutions 1 & 2 `20s`

**[Fragment trigger: The graph connections disappear, leaving isolated floating points.]**

> "Maybe our first instinct is: let's just ignore the relationships.
>
> Now every node is just an isolated piece of data."

*(Beat.)*

> "Except we've thrown away the very information we cared about."

**[Fragment trigger: An adjacency matrix appears.]**

> "Fine. Then let's keep the relationships.
> We'll store them in an adjacency matrix and hand that matrix to a neural network.
>
> That looks completely reasonable."

*(Beat.)*

> "Until you watch this."

---

## 2:25 — Scene 6 · The Failure: Permutation Sensitivity `35s`

**[Two caffeine molecules shown side by side. Same atoms, same bonds.]**

> "Same molecule.
> Same atoms.
> Same bonds."

**[Numbers change on the nodes. Two matrices appear below with different rows/columns.]**

> "The only thing I changed...
> is which atom I decided to label as number one."

**[Fragment trigger: Predictions flash: Toxic 0.83 vs Toxic 0.11. Pulsing Red X stamp slams in.]**

> "Toxic: 0.83.
> Toxic: 0.11.
>
> Same molecule. Different answer."

*(Hold the silence for two full seconds. Do not rush this punchline.)*

> "The neural network learned our numbering system.
> It never learned the chemistry."

---

## 3:00 — Scene 7 · The Pivotal Question `25s` · **PAUSE POINT**

**[Screen clears to a single glowing node.]**

> "So here is the real question."

**[Large bold text: What is this node?]**

*(Pause.)*

> "Maybe we look at its features. Age, size, color..."

**[Animation: The word 'is' is struck through and replaced in glowing gold: Who influences this node?]**

*(Stop talking. Count to two.)*

> "One small change. Completely different problem.
>
> Now the node cannot be understood by looking only at itself.
> We have to look at who it is connected to.
>
> And that is the question ordinary neural networks were never designed to ask."

---

## 3:25 — Scene 8 · The GNN Idea `35s`

**[A central node with neighbours appears.]**

> "So what if...
> instead of forcing the graph to behave like an image grid,
> we build a neural network that understands the graph itself?
>
> Start with one node.
> Ask the people it is connected to for information."

**[Animation: Envelopes travel along incident edges inward.]**

> "**Gather.**
> Its neighbours send what they know."

**[Animation: Envelopes collapse into one box.]**

> "**Aggregate.**
> We combine what they told us — without caring who spoke first."

**[Animation: Node changes color/state.]**

> "**Update.**
> And now the node has a new representation of itself.
>
> And every node on the board does this at the exact same time.
>
> That is the basic idea of a **Graph Neural Network**."

---

## 4:00 — Scene 9 · Step-by-Step GNN Demo: Alice's Graph `95s`

**[A clean, named 5-node graph: Alice connected to Bob and Carol; David connected to Bob; Eve connected to Carol.]**

> "Let's actually run one together, step by step.
>
> Suppose these nodes are people in a network.
> And our question is:
>
> *Is Alice likely to be interested in Topic X?*"

**[Alice is highlighted.]**

> "First, Alice looks at herself.
> Her profile says: Age 24, Activity High, Topic Interest: Unknown.
> Her own card isn't enough to answer the question."

**[Bob and Carol are highlighted in gold.]**

> "So Alice looks around. Step one: **Gather**.
> She asks her immediate neighbours, Bob and Carol."

**[Animation: Bob and Carol's feature cards physically slide along the edges toward Alice.]**

> "Bob sends his card: he loves Topic X.
> Carol sends hers: she follows related discussions.
>
> The messages arrive at Alice."

**[Animation: The arriving cards merge into an aggregation box.]**

> "Step two: **Aggregate**.
> We combine what they told Alice.
> It doesn't matter whether Bob's message arrived first or Carol's arrived first.
> The combination is order-independent."

**[Animation: Alice's card flips to a new color and status.]**

> "Step three: **Update**.
> Alice takes what her neighbours told her, combines it with her own information,
> and updates her card.
>
> Her interest is no longer unknown. It flips to: **Likely Interested: 89%**."

**[Zoom out: The entire graph ripples and all nodes update simultaneously.]**

> "And every node on the board just did that at the exact same time.
>
> The whole graph is learning through conversations."

---

## 5:35 — Scene 10 · The Intuitive Equation `25s`

**[The GNN message-passing equation appears in the center.]**

> "Now we can look at the formal equation.
> But do not be intimidated by this notation —
> because this equation is not a new concept.
>
> It is simply the animation you just watched, written down."

**[Runtime SVG callouts connect each term to plain English labels.]**

> "Look at the pieces:
> My neighbours.
> The shuffle-proof combiner.
> Combine with the old card.
> Produce the new card.
>
> So whenever you see this equation in a paper, think:
> **Gather. Aggregate. Update.**"

---

## 6:00 — Scene 11 · Depth = Reach & Over-smoothing `40s`

**[Graph layout with Layer counter in the corner.]**

> "Now one question appears naturally.
>
> If one layer lets Alice hear from her direct neighbours..."

**[Layer 1 counter. 1-hop ring glows gold.]**

> "What happens if we stack a second layer?"

**[Layer 2 counter. 2-hop ring glows.]**

> "Now Alice can hear from her neighbours' neighbours — David and Eve."

**[Layer 3 counter. 3-hop ring glows.]**

> "Three layers? Three steps away.
>
> So should we just keep adding dozens of layers like we do in CNNs?"

*(Pause.)*

> "No."

**[Layer counter jumps to 6. Warp pulse. All nodes fade to identical uniform gray.]**

> "Interrogate everyone long enough, and eventually everyone hears the exact same story.
> Every node becomes identical.
>
> That is called **over-smoothing**.
>
> Most production GNNs use only two or three layers — and now you understand why."

---

## 6:40 — Scene 12 · Not All Informants Are Equal `30s`

**[The graph edges change thickness and glow.]**

> "There is one more question.
> In our demo, Alice listened to Bob and Carol equally.
>
> But should she?
>
> What if Bob is a verified subject-matter expert, and Carol is a stranger?"

**[One edge turns thick and gold; another turns thin and dim.]**

> "Maybe the network should learn to pay more attention to one neighbour than another.
>
> That single idea gives rise to attention-based GNNs like **GAT**.
>
> Average everyone: that's **GCN**.
> Sample a few: that's **GraphSAGE**.
> Learn who to trust: that's **GAT**.
>
> Not every relationship is equally useful."

---

## 7:10 — Scene 13 · Case Study 1: Halicin (Drug Discovery) `50s`

**[Background changes to electron micrograph of bacteria. Case card appears.]**

> "Up to this point, this has been an elegant theoretical idea.
>
> So what happens when people actually put it to work?
>
> What if the graph is a molecule?
> Atoms are nodes. Bonds are edges.
>
> Now the neural network can reason about chemical connectivity instead of an arbitrary matrix."

**[Fragment trigger: Details of MIT 2020 study appear.]**

> "In 2020, researchers at MIT used this to screen a digital library of roughly 100 million compounds.
>
> The model flagged a molecule that had been completely overlooked as an antibiotic.
> They named it **halicin**.
>
> Halicin killed strains of bacteria resistant to every known antibiotic,
> including *Acinetobacter baumannii* on screen behind me.
>
> A neural network read chemistry as a graph and found a life-saving drug humans had walked right past."

---

## 8:00 — Scene 14 · Case Study 2: GraphCast (Global Weather) `40s`

**[Satellite image of Earth overlaid with an atmospheric mesh graph.]**

> "Now let's expand the scale from nanometers to an entire planet.
>
> What if every point on Earth is a node?
> Nearby points in the atmosphere are connected by edges.
>
> The atmosphere becomes one colossal graph."

**[Fragment trigger: GraphCast results.]**

> "This is DeepMind's **GraphCast**.
>
> It generates a 10-day global weather forecast in **under a minute on a single machine**.
>
> It outperformed the European gold-standard supercomputer model that takes hours to run,
> and it predicted the trajectory of Hurricane Lee days earlier.
>
> Same principle. Planetary scale."

---

## 8:40 — Scene 15 · Case Study 3: CERN LHC (Particle Tracking) `40s`

**[CMS/ATLAS particle detector collision visual.]**

> "And one more.
>
> Inside the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, each proton collision sprays thousands of sensor hits.
>
> The challenge is: which hits came from the same subatomic particle?"

**[Fragment trigger: Hit cloud to candidate graph to pruned tracks.]**

> "It's a connect-the-dots problem at an absurd physical scale.
>
> Physicists build a graph of candidate hits and let a GNN prune the false connections,
> reconstructing particle trajectories in milliseconds.
>
> Graphs are being used to discover new physics."

---

## 9:20 — Scene 16 · The Unifying Picture `25s`

**[Three cards side by side: Molecule, Planet Earth, Particle Detector.]**

> "A molecule.
> A planet.
> A particle detector.
>
> On the surface, these three fields have absolutely nothing in common."

**[Animation: The three images fade away into darkness, leaving only one word glowing in gold: GRAPH]**

> "Except underneath... they have the identical structure.
>
> Things connected to other things.
>
> **Different problems. Same structure.**"

---

## 9:45 — Scene 17 · Return to Opening & Final Close `15s`

**[The opening Dhaka suspect card and its network return to screen.]**

> "Remember the person we started with ten minutes ago?
>
> We couldn't understand them from their own profile alone.
> We had to look at their network.
>
> Whenever the relationships are part of the information...
> ignoring those relationships is throwing information away.
>
> A Graph Neural Network is simply the way we teach neural networks...
> to look at the things... and the connections...
> at the exact same time."

*(Pause. Hold the visual.)*

> "Thank you."
